td 2026-08-22: CAPTURE CEILING — the display track is now constrained to the export size (1920×1080@30, tied to DEFAULT_EXPORT_SETTINGS via CAPTURE_MAX_* in acquire.ts) instead of taking whatever the surface offers. Trigger: PO recorded a Chrome tab rendering a 4K game and "the video freezes" — which also answers the open question left by the 2026-08-06 frozen-source work (the shared surface was ONE tab, and the take was not merely a still image: frames arrive in bursts). Root cause is throughput, not liveness: a 4K surface is consumed FOUR times over on one GPU — Chrome's capture pipeline reads back 3840×2160 frames, the raw screen MediaRecorder H.264-encodes them at 8 Mbps (0.03 bpp), the live composite decodes them into a <video> and downscales to 1080p 30×/s, and the on-screen preview decodes the same stream again — while the captured tab renders the game on that same GPU. Every one of those pixels is discarded at export, which is 1080p on both paths. MEASURED (real Chrome, hidden tab, AudioWorklet-driven rig mirroring liveComposite: source canvas → captureStream → <video> → drawImage → 1080p canvas, two concurrent 8 Mbps MediaRecorders, 8s per trial, 2 runs with order reversed, idle machine, no real screen capture and no game): 4K source delivered 21.5 / 11.9 fps against a 30 fps target with drawImage at 13.94 / 8.55 ms per frame (of a 33 ms budget) and the composite encoder starved to 289 / 106 KB/s; the same rig at 1080p delivered 29.8–29.9 fps, drawImage 0.97–1.04 ms, encoders 597–614 KB/s. So the cap buys 8–14× cheaper compositing and restores full frame delivery, at zero quality cost — those 4K pixels never reached the file. Constraints are MAX-only (a smaller surface satisfies them untouched, so screen capture can never be overconstrained away), applied in the getDisplayMedia request AND re-applied via capDisplayTrack() before the channel is delivered — i.e. before its MediaRecorder exists, since a resolution change after recorder.start() reinitialises the encoder mid-file; applyConstraints is bounded (1.5s) and failure-tolerant (an oversized take beats no take). frameRate is capped as MAX, not merely ideal: a 60 fps game tab otherwise hands over 60 fps, all encoded twice and then dropped at export. Also added: per-source delivered-fps logged every 10s from the composite tick (console only — a static screen legitimately delivers ~1 keep-alive fps, so low cadence cannot be judged as a fault here; this exists to turn the next "it stutters" report into a number). PO frozen rule respected — additive, nothing that worked was removed.
